[{"content": "You are a coding agent working on a bug-repair task. Use repository tools to inspect, edit, and run checks. The task environment contains a writable pinned repository. The task repository is the only mounted host directory.\n\nAvailable tools:\n- read_file: Read a UTF-8 file from the task repository. Arguments: path.\n- search_text: Search repository text, case-insensitively. Arguments: query.\n- apply_patch: Create a file with path/content, or replace one exact old_text match with new_text.\n- run_shell: Run a shell command from the task repository root. Arguments: command.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Task description:\n# Repair title-only retrieval relevance\n\nThe tutor's top-k retrieval can omit a useful section when the question matches\nthe section heading but the section body does not repeat those words. The\nproblem is visible in the selected passages and in the source text sent to the\ntutor model.\n\nMake the relevant section available when its heading matches the question, even\nwhen an unrelated section appears first and `k=1`. Keep body-text relevance and\nthe existing top-k, case-normalization, input-immutability, full-context,\nhistory, and mode behavior intact.\n\nThe task is limited to the retrieval and tutor-context behavior in\n`app/source.py` and `app/tutor.py`. A fake tutor response is supplied by the\noffline checks; no model service or network access is needed.\n\n\nRepository instructions (AGENTS.md):\n# AGENTS.md\n\nConventions for any coding assistant (GitHub Copilot in VS Code, Codespaces or\nthe CLI) working in this repository.\n\n## What this project is\nAn ML tutor chat for classmates: it answers questions on linear/softmax\nregression, weight decay, gradient descent, perceptrons, MLPs and\nbackpropagation, citing a section id like `[3.7.1]` for every fact. One\nStreamlit screen, nothing else: no login, no settings, no database, no user\naccounts. If a change adds a second screen, stop and ask.\n\n## Layout\n- `app/tutor.py` holds `answer(question, ...)`, the one job. The screen and the evals both call it.\n- `app/source.py` loads `source/sections.jsonl`, retrieves sections (BM25) and parses citations.\n- `app/llm.py` is the only file that talks to a model (NVIDIA, OpenAI-compatible). Keep tutor and judge on separate model families; IDs are configured in `.env.example`.\n- `prompts/tutor_vN.md` holds the tutor's prompts. Never edit an evaluated prompt: copy to the next version.\n- `source/` is the only text the tutor may teach from (CC BY-SA, see `source/ATTRIBUTION.md`).\n- `evals/` holds the golden set, code checks, the LLM judge and its rubric and calibration, results and the metric write-up.\n- `docs/` holds the iteration log and the report and slide outlines.\n\n## Rules\n- Synthetic or public text only. Never add real personal data to any file.\n- Never add text from Géron's book (or any copyrighted source) to the repo; this repo goes public.\n- Never commit `.env` or any API key.\n- Never change the expected sections or key facts in `evals/golden_set.jsonl`, or the\n  human labels in `evals/rubric/calibration.jsonl`, to make a score go up. Fix the app or the rubric instead.\n- Every evaluated change gets an entry in `docs/iteration_log.md`, including the ones we abandon.\n- Keep it small enough to demo in ninety seconds. Polish is not marked.\n- Run `python -m unittest` before committing; it needs no API key.\n\n## Skills\n`.github/skills/` holds the skills Copilot can load. `run-evals` is this\nproject's own procedure; the rest are Matt Pocock's engineering skills\n(MIT, see `.github/skills/LICENSE-mattpocock-skills`). Useful ones here:\n`tdd` for eval code, `grilling` to stress-test the idea, `code-review` before\na submission commit, `diagnosing-bugs` when the demo breaks.\n\n\nRepository file map:\n- .env.example\n- .gitignore\n- AGENTS.md\n- README.md\n- requirements.txt\n- app/__init__.py\n- app/llm.py\n- app/source.py\n- app/streamlit_app.py\n- app/tutor.py\n- tes